`TestParseStatementsReadsMariaDBOnlySyntax` and `TestGetStatementTypesClassifiesMariaDBOnlyStatements` cover the Bytebase side of this — that MariaDB routing reaches the MariaDB parser and classifies its own syntax.

**`CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE` now reports affected rows.** It drops the existing table first, so its rows are affected, but the extractor passed `false` for every create — the MySQL grammar it was forked from has no such form. Before this PR the statement was skipped and no figure was published at all; without the fix it would have published zero. An approval rule keyed on `statement.affected_rows` can now fire on a replacement create where it previously saw nothing.

**MariaDB-only DML now gets a real affected-rows estimate.** The plancheck summary routed MARIADB to the MySQL driver's `CountAffectedRows`, which parsed with `ParseMySQL` — so a `DELETE ... RETURNING` never reached an EXPLAIN and the summary published zero r

